{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The min-cost fixed point over integer costs does not preserve reinstatement as a derived property. Reinstatement requires that once an attacker is refuted, its target is accepted, and later re-refutation of the refuter re-reinstates the target. Under cost-bounded defense, the least fixed point is monotone in the 'chosen defeats' lattice, so a previously accepted node never gets removed; but the target's acceptance also depends on *its own* attackers' refutations being funded within their budgets at each stage. Once a budget is consumed by a particular defeat-set selection to refute an attacker, re-analysis at a later stage may find a different allocation is required, yet the lattice only grows — it never re-allocates. Consequently, a node reinstated in the classical cycle is not guaranteed acceptance because the cost-budget state is sticky, not genuinely cyclical: reinstatement is not derivable.","target":"e03422c06d13666a784d90f5c6f63d998c82fb3fcaa2c200c760a61a088dbb10"}]}